Title:
KKYUMPoC — PoC and analysis of KKYUM.sys WHQL-signed cheat driver (arbitrary kernel read/write) + unelevated chain via eneio64 (CVE-2020-12446)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository provides a driver analysis and local privilege escalation PoC for KKYUM.sys, a WHQL-attestation signed cheat driver exposing a permissive `\\.\KKYUM` device with weak/no ACL.  
- The driver wraps `MmCopyVirtualMemory` behind IOCTLs that accept a caller-supplied PID, enabling arbitrary cross-process read/write (including kernel virtual addresses) from a standard user context once the driver is loaded.  
- The included `pwn.c` demonstrates a data-only token-steal (EPROCESS traversal + token swap) to spawn a SYSTEM shell, compatible with HVCI, using kernel base discovery via `NtQuerySystemInformation(SystemModuleInformation)` (requires enabled `SeDebugPrivilege`).  
- `chain_exploit.c` removes the admin/SeDebug requirement by chaining `eneio64.sys` (CVE-2020-12446) to map physical memory and defeat KASLR by scanning low physical memory for the kernel entry pointer, then using KKYUM’s primitive to locate `PsInitialSystemProcess` and steal SYSTEM’s token.  
- It’s useful for red teams and exploit developers studying BYOVD-style LPE, kernel address disclosure mitigations on Win11, and practical KASLR bypasses under modern scrubbing/decoy behaviors.  
- Defensive notes include blocking by driver hash and highlighting that neither driver was present in Microsoft’s vulnerable driver blocklist at the time described.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) and abuse of WHQL-attestation signed drivers
- IOCTL design flaws (caller-controlled PID) enabling arbitrary read/write via `MmCopyVirtualMemory`
- Windows kernel LPE via EPROCESS list walk and token stealing (DKOM-style token swap)
- Kernel base/KASLR bypass via physical memory mapping (eneio64 `\\.\GLCKIo`, IOCTL `0x80102040`)
- Windows 11 kernel pointer disclosure mitigations/scrubbing and decoy IDT base behavior (`sidt` trap)

Use Cases:
- Validate and reproduce LPE risk from permissive third-party kernel drivers in an environment
- Develop/benchmark kernel token-steal primitives using arbitrary read/write
- Research KASLR bypass techniques when `NtQuerySystemInformation` outputs are scrubbed
- Build detections for suspicious driver service creation/start and access to vulnerable device objects
- Support vulnerable-driver blocklist testing and incident response triage for BYOVD activity

Keywords:
KKYUM.sys, BYOVD, WHQL attestation, LOLDrivers, IOCTL, MmCopyVirtualMemory, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, NtQuerySystemInformation, SystemModuleInformation, KASLR bypass, physical memory mapping, eneio64.sys, CVE-2020-12446, GLCKIo, EPROCESS, ActiveProcessLinks, token stealing, HVCI, VBS, driversipolicy.p7b